# Review package: batch 3, slice delta

## 92e1bf6 Refuse a late relay write before it lands, and let one bad value or a failed rollback harm nobody else

Batch 3's fix round, from its one review (runs/batch3-merged.md). Each fix started from a test that failed first.

A refused registration could land: its statement was claimed into a commit, its call's budget ended, and plugin('NewProxy') refused while the commit moved the generation. The name then read held by the refused client for 10 s, and routes.generation parted from sync.generation, which stops a standby's takeover at check 5. A ticket now carries its deadline, claim_statements refuses a late one before its SQL runs, and a claimed write waits for its commit's outcome. A NewProxy marks last_seen before its rule, because a write after the registration could still refuse a registration already on disk, which the review's fix alone left open.

A failed ROLLBACK left a live transaction on a kept-alive thread: later writes answered 200 with nothing on disk and held the write lock against everyone. A connection still in a transaction after ROLLBACK is closed and dropped, write() knows `with transaction()` by a thread-local flag, and commit_batch catches BaseException, so no ticket goes back uncommitted with no error.

One client's value rolled back other companies' writes: a lone surrogate or an int too large raises outside sqlite3.Error. WriteTicket.run now catches per statement, and sync_text refuses a lone surrogate, so POST /sync answers 400 invalid.

Read-only admin commands took the write lock seven times each, so under a long commit hornhoof-relay ban could mark a name banned and fail before it rewrote blocked.caddy. init_db reads sqlite_master first and writes only what is missing, and a busy store is one plain line for the operator.

The failure on 3.14 was not frps answering slowly: frps exited at once, "bind: address already in use" on its vhost port. A Stack releases the ports it picks until their owners start, and the echo's bind(0) could draw one. The echo now binds a picked port, and a process that loses a port is told who holds it.

Minor: try right after wait_for_turn in transaction(), write()'s docstring on -1, and the refused-on-time test asserts its timing.

Ruling: a plugin write claimed into a commit waits for that commit's outcome, past its budget, overturning the writer's ruling 3 - why: its SQL may land, so a refusal there can be false, and that broke takeover - cost if wrong: on a stalled disk one plugin call answers late by the length of the stall, once, since its next write refuses at write_deadline and the calls queued behind still refuse at their own budgets.
Ruling: only a NewProxy marks last_seen before its rule; Login and Ping keep it last - why: nothing may follow the registration's write, and test_login_run_id_read_with_a_spent_budget_never_reaches_the_dashboard pins Login's order - cost if wrong: an authenticated client's refused NewProxy moves last_seen, at most once per 600 s.
Ruling: test_a_plugin_call_stops_waiting_for_a_commit_that_outlives_its_budget gave way to test_every_write_in_a_commit_under_way_waits_for_its_outcome, an old test changed - why: it pinned ruling 3, overturned, and its API half stays pinned in the new one - cost if wrong: none beyond the first ruling.
Ruling: the other owners of a commit torn down by a BaseException hear WriteBusy, not that exception - why: it is the holder thread's own, and busy is true since nothing landed - cost if wrong: a caller retries a commit that failed for a graver reason.
Ruling: the plain line for a busy store is in a new run_admin that main calls, not in admin() - why: admin() scores over CRAP 10 and splitting it is no part of this fix - cost if wrong: an in-process caller of admin() still sees WriteBusy; only main calls it.
Ruling: no catch for a bad value in upsert_route, where the review proposed one - why: the merged list asks for the per-statement catch, and handle_plugin already refuses what plugin() raises, with the route lock released in a finally - cost if wrong: frpc shows relay error: UnicodeEncodeError in place of a friendlier reason.
Ruling: the echo's bind(0) is named as the cause of frps's lost port, without the log naming the holder - why: a run's container has its own network, so only its own processes can hold the port; 34537 is odd, and Linux gives odd ports to bind(0) and even to connect; the echo is the one bind(0) held across that window; and on a-host bind(0) drew one of six released ports in 6 of 20000 tries - cost if wrong: the failure returns, and its error now names the holder. On a-host lanes share one network, so another lane's run can still draw a port in that window.

